This tutorial demonstrates how to use Godot's built-in serialization module and YAMLParser class to load external behavior tree configuration files. Each configuration will define its own root node with various tasks and sequences within the tree.


Using external YAML files allows for better version control and reusability of behavior trees across project iterations. It also enables easy modifications of existing behavior trees without requiring code changes.


## Usage

1. Install gdscript_yaml (`git clone https://github.com/darkroyal/gdscript_yaml.git && cd gdscript_yaml && cp src/*.gdnlib ~/.godot/bin/x64/plugins/`) if not already done.
2. Create YAML files in data folder named as required. Their names must follow the convention of `<behavior_tree_name>.yml`.
3. Import these files into your project by adding corresponding directories under `res://data/<behavior_tree_name>/<file>.yml`
4. Instantiate a BehaviorTreeManager node in a scene alongside a BehaviorTreeRunner or BehaviorTreeRunnerMulti node.

```
extends Node2D

var behavior_tree_manager : BehaviorTreeManager setget set_behavior_tree_manager, get_behavior_tree_manager
var runner : BehaviorTreeRunner

func _ready():
    pass

func set_behavior_tree_manager(value):
    behavior_tree_manager = value

func get_behavior_tree_manager():
     return behavior_tree_manager

func setup_behavior_tree(runner, bt_name):
    var yml_file_path = "res://data/" + bt_name + "/" + bt_name + ".yml"
    var parser = YamlParser.new()

    var yml_file = File.new()
    yml_file.open(yml_file_path, File.READ)
    var config_data = parse_json(yml_file.get_as_text())
    yml_file.close()

    var tree_def = {}
    if parser.parse(config_data.to_string(), tree_def):
        var manager = BehaviorTreeManager.new()
        manager.root_node = BT.load(bt_def)
        runner.tree_root = manager.root_node

